ade and labeling up  a sample we might not always have  detected it at our  specimen reception bearing in mind the  amount of workload that we were handling  at any one time  so further progress that has been made  on the new automation things that are  new to us in this last year with our new  incarnation of automation  is looking more at the host analytical  so samples going back to cold storage  units online called storage units  so really touch time for the sample is  minimal  the samples are loaded on every step is  taken care of by the automation  i described already how we send referral  samples to referral labs  so the new generation of our automation  has got modules that allow alloquotes to  be made  online so this the alicorta unit will  generate a daughter chief it will  produce a label and so it will label  the daughter tube and the olive will be  placed in it so then all the member of  staff needs to do is  access that sample take it away and send  it on to the next lab  there's so much time saved by removing  repetitive manual tasks  and that is what the automation does for  you it also lets us have staff  as we mentioned at the beginning of the  presentation both haematology and  biochemistry  have low and working members of staff  who work overnight they are able to  criticize far more  inpatient samples that come during an  evening during the night shift  than if they had to do everything  manually and as paul is described as  they decide there's  a rerun to be done or an issue with an  instrument they can  schedule all of that from one location  rather than having to move around  getting samples and hunting things down  another added benefit  is that because we know the automation  will will robustly process  a certain number of samples within a  certain time we can't predict our  turnaround times more  accurately so if a gp wants to know if  his blood sample that he sent in on a  monday afternoon will be ready by a  tuesday morning  we can give him an honest answer to that  question that yes it probably will be  so let's go back to that previous slide  again just to build on that as well from  a hematology perspective and we've tried  to eliminate some of the steps that  we were still having to do even with our  previous automated track  and that was mainly looking at the pre  and post analytical  so we've introduced a recap around to  the track we've introduced  a d capper on the track as well so for  all those hematitic samples that we're  having to  to recap and people complaining about  their thumbs and having to stick all  these caps on  that's no longer an issue and we also  make the decision to  track our coagulation analyzers as well  so  you'll see there's a lady in the  distance there and suitably face mast as  well she would say  in that area and this picture was taken  about three months ago now  but just in the last six weeks we've  introduced our two  coagulation analyzers into that area as  well so samples no longer have to be  spun  offline they can be spun on the track  although we do still  spin the specialist coagulation offline  so again  just eliminating a lot of manual steps  there  one other things again because this is  fairly new to us as you can see i mean  we only started  going live on what was that three weeks  ago in hematology with the automated  track  not long before that for biochemistry so  this is very new automation to ourselves  as well  and we're still to build on this  automation another thing that we'll be  bringing in  hopefully end of this year beginning of  next year will be a refrigerated storage  module  so those samples will automatically go  from the track into the storage module  again these can be recalled if we wanted  to reflect samples  but they the added advantage there is we  can set time lights  so for example after two days we can say  that all the edta samples for full blood  cams  can be removed so they will  automatically be removed  into a multi-cloud bag ready for the  support worker just to pick up  and dispose of so no longer are we  having to go into fridges  empty rack after a rack of samples into  the autoclave  the automation will now deliver that for  us  we also will intend to have well we do  have two monitors  in each of the laboratories as well one  monitor will show you  the track and the and how things are  behaving how the analyzers are behaving  etc  so you can see that it's quite visible  from anywhere within the laboratory  the other monitor will show us our  turnaround times and we'll highlight if  they  were in danger of breaching at the  turnaround time for a  e or for amir also with the tracker we  have we now have priority lanes so we  can actually prioritize  our e a under the old system we were  unable to do that  but we can't truly prioritize our any  samples now  so just  so what we've done is we've fully  automated the the technical process  we've automated the pre-analytical and  we've managed to automate the  post-analytical process as well  but we still felt in hematology and  biochemistry there was an area that we  could improve upon  and that was when samples were being  delivered to the laboratory  so basically our sample reception area  up until about six months ago each  sample that was  that was delivered to the laboratories  came in an individual plastic sample bag  that bag had to be opened up we would  then scan the barcode label that was on  the sample  that would bring up the patient  demographics we would check that  against the properties being shown on  the computer and at that point we would  then  relabel the sample so we'd have a unique  laboratory identifier barcode label  and we would put that in the sample that  would then have to be scanned  and then we'd order the tests so you can  see a lot of manual  repetitive steps involved there in  booking in  samples we had discussed run through  labeling for a number of years  but because the limitations of our lym