t in front of  the sensor  and placing it in the rack then you  could see the advantages  because of that waste of plastic and  eliminating plastic waste  our couriers will then come along pick  up those racks  and deliver them to appropriate  department so they would come into the  hematology and you can see  data we have here in that we have four  cradles that could be  scaled up it could be scaled down  depending on the on the workload  but we have four cradles here so when  the samples are delivered  we can place four four rats into these  cradles  we then go to the the pc and we would in  effect  accept those samples in and within  seconds  every single sample is within those rack  is booked into our limb system  so you can see how this is quite  groundbreaking for us in that we have  eliminated so many manual steps at some  perception  from these samples are being delivered  in this particular way  as we said we introduced this to 12  pilot sites in july  we then rolled that out to another 24  sites  in august again those that were sending  in the most samples  we felt there was no point in sending a  sample to gp surgery  and there are some of them who are  sending in maybe two samples a day  we didn't think it required it but we  still have some racks that we can put  out if we decide to  increase the number still so that's now  36 practices  into the 92 that have this system in  place  and although that's only 40 of the  practices at still  over 60 percent of the workload that's  now coming into the department  is now boot in in this way  and if we look at this graph here this  just highlights just how many samples  are coming in from primary care  in hematology in biochemistry so you can  see fifteen hundred  averaging fifteen hundred for hematology  about two two and a half thousand for  biochemistry but even allowing for sixty  percent of that coming in in this  fashion you're saying that's about 900  hematology samples  and about 13 1400 biochemistry sa