m automation so we have the same  middleware so we can look at their cases  on their analyzers  and we can compare how they are  performing against the analyzers of  aberdeen  both for hematology and for clinical  biochemistry  this is important from a newcastle  interview as well if you have a patient  who's admitted to drugs and then  transferred to eri  you want to know that the results are  compatible so all through our assessment  visit visits the automation have  helped us achieve our accreditation  status  thank you anne and looks like we have  one more question here  you started very early with automation  in 2008  why did you decide to automate so early  any recommendation for someone who wants  to start automating now  i think that sounds like a question from  the answer i'll say that  in 2008 for you as you've finally said  so  so why did we go in 2008 i took over the  laboratory modules posts in hematology  in 2005  so we started the procurement process  the following year  for for the new analyzers that we wanted  to go to  and i always remember being the lone  voice at the time saying that we should  we should actually automate and i was  trying to make the point  it wasn't about just looking for what we  needed now  this was about trying to future proof  for the next 10 years  because quite often that procurement  process is for a 10 year  plus in this case it was 12 years before  we actually went to  our finally got analyzers back in again  so you do have to have that long-term  approach to what is required  one of my mantras of course as well was  making sure we got the right person to  do the right job  and an upskilling staff as well so  i didn't want to have biomedical  scientists breaking through racks trying  to find a sample  which happened in a daily basis because  somebody would come in and just put a  sample down  in the first space they could find in  iraq and inevitably that was the sample  you needed for a blood film or for a pb  for exa